WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.520
- [ATC Controller] Delta 1943
cancel takeoff clearance.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.170
- [Narrator] So far this
year there have been

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.150
at least six aircraft close calls

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:06.983
that have rattled

00:00:06.983 --> 00:00:09.030
both aviation officials and travelers.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:09.990
- [Operator] You have the runway yet?

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.920
- [Pilot] We're going around.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.753
- [Operator 2] Line up and wait,

00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:13.038
traffic rolling across the runway.

00:00:13.038 --> 00:00:14.220
JetBlue 206, go around.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.380
- [Narrator] Some safety
officials say the increase

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.300
in collision warnings might be explained

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.000
in part by pilots and
air traffic controllers

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.940
grappling with the rise
in postpandemic traffic.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.200
Here's a breakdown of one

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.910
of this year's near collisions

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.280
and what the busiest
airport in the world does

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.023
to prevent close calls like it.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.050
On February 4th

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.300
at Austin Bergstrom International Airport,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.010
a FedEx plane was cleared to land

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.140
around 6:40 AM local time.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.780
It was still mostly dark
and visibility was poor

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.130
due to weather conditions.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.010
When the FedEx plane was
approximately three miles away,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.590
an air traffic controller
cleared a Southwest flight

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.480
to depart that same runway.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.070
As the FedEx plane was landing,

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.650
its pilots noticed the
Southwest flight was still

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.483
on the runway.

00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:58.080
- [Operator 3] Southwest board.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.130
FedEx is on the go.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.680
- [Narrator] So the FedEx
pilots initiated a go-around

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.050
and safely landed 12 minutes later.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.880
But this could have been a disaster.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.210
The Southwest flight
had 128 people on board.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.010
A FedEx spokeswoman said the company

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.930
appreciated the efforts
from its flight crew

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.000
to land the Austin flight safely.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.530
And in a message to employees,

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.690
Southwest Chief Executive Bob Jordan

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.160
commended the Southwest crew

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.110
and said the airline
would fully participate

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.700
in the investigation.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.030
The FAA and NTSB are
investigating six serious

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.620
runway incursions that have occurred

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.150
so far this year.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.090
These incidents show how
important air traffic control is

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.530
in keeping planes safe.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.330
So to understand how it works,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.403
we went to the world's busiest airport.

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:42.214
- Delta 2899, give way

00:01:42.214 --> 00:01:43.920
to the inbound RJ at five.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.570
- [Narrator] This is the
air traffic control tower

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.420
at Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta
International Airport.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.950
It's responsible for the airspace

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.980
approximately five
miles out and 4,000 feet

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.240
above the airport.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.530
- If we're really busy

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.480
and you're working the
clear takeoff runway,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.010
you could have 20 airplanes in line

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.990
that are all on your frequency at once.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.210
- [Narrator] Drew Buckman has
been an air traffic controller

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.830
for almost 18 years.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.240
- [Drew] You're looking for
those little abnormalities

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.130
here and there, and you
can't help it anymore.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.230
It's just what you're trained to do here.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.210
- [Narrator] Air traffic
control towers are managed

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.380
by the FAA.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.540
Atlanta's Tower operates 24X7

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.710
due to its high volume.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.290
But smaller airports
sometimes go uncontrolled

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.030
when volume is low though pilots

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.020
can still communicate

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.420
with the terminal radar
approach control facility

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.270
or an air route traffic
control center, if needed.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.980
Controllers in Atlanta juggle

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.930
around 2100 flights a day

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.190
so they're only allowed to work

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.380
in the tower for two hours at a time.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.790
There are three main positions

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.770
that controllers work in Atlanta's Tower.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.780
- [Controller 1] The local
control are the tower controllers

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.070
that are working airplanes

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.660
on each individual runway.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.940
Delta 398, tower runway
two six left full length,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.860
line up and wait, traffic is downfield.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.270
- Ground controllers are doing

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.410
what the title suggests

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.242
and you're working
airplanes on the ground.

00:02:51.242 --> 00:02:53.400
- Delta 2694 Atlanta ground.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.510
- And then we do have a third position

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.070
which is called clearance delivery

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:56.940
and they're the ones

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.130
who are giving the
clearances to the pilots,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.470
either electronically or by voice.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.910
- [Narrator] Here's what
that process looks like

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.713
for a plane departing from Atlanta.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.120
When an aircraft is ready to taxi,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.820
clearance delivery creates
a flight progress strip

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.620
that has key information like the type

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.690
of aircraft and its departure gate.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.880
- [Drew] The proposed time is here.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.080
It is in Zulu time.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.550
- [Narrator] Which is
the standard time used

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.740
across the aviation industry.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.030
- [Drew] Its requested final altitude

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.860
is gonna be 37,000 feet.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.690
- [Narrator] All this
information helps controllers

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.340
keep track of where every plane

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.570
at the airport is going

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.100
to avoid collisions.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.800
Next, clearance delivery
slides that strip over

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.050
to the ground controller,

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.040
who puts the scripts in a sequence.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.910
This is the order in
which planes will line

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:42.870
up on the runway.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.580
- [Drew] Once they get
the aircraft taxied down

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.650
and it's in the order
they want it to be in,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.660
then they'll switch it
over to local control

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.830
- [Narrator] Who will scan the barcode

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:50.910
on the flight strip

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.653
and clear the plane for takeoff.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.920
Before controllers can
work with real planes

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.660
in the tower, they spend a six to

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.483
eight weeks training
here in the simulator.

00:04:00.483 --> 00:04:02.730
(indistinct chatter)

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.110
- Southwest 1042 contact-

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.160
- [Narrator] Where they practice working

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.990
in all kinds of weather conditions,

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.670
like rain and fog.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.930
- [Drew] In those foggy situations,

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.640
when I can't see out the window,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.200
this radar here is gonna gimme the tools

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.700
that I need to be able to see

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.530
what's actually happening
outside of the tower.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.170
- [Narrator] Controllers
also use this airborne radar

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.850
to monitor planes in the air.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.440
- [Drew] What we look
at here, it's telling us

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.820
which runway they're gonna land on,

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.610
the type of aircraft, the altitude,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.830
and the speed of each aircraft as well.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.450
- [Narrator] This
information helps controllers

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.210
keep aircraft a safe
distance apart in the air

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.220
somewhere between three and eight miles,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.200
depending on the weight of the aircraft.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.270
In that near collision in Austin, Texas,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.320
the two planes ended

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.750
up approximately 100 feet from each other

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.160
according to preliminary data

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.460
from the National
Transportation Safety Board.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.597
In all situations, the FAA says,

00:04:49.597 --> 00:04:51.180
"Communication between pilots

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.095
and air traffic controllers is key."

00:04:53.095 --> 00:04:55.428
(crosstalk)

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.430
Pilots and air traffic controllers have

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.810
to be proficient in the use

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.360
of the International Civil
Aviation Organization's

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.770
standard phraseology.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.610
- [Drew] The typical ones are clear

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.443
for takeoff and clear land.

00:05:06.443 --> 00:05:07.440
Those are the ones people know.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.500
Go around means cancel
your approach clearance,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.790
we're gonna send you around

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.410
and you're gonna come back and land again.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.840
- [Narrator] Some commands
require a specific response

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:16.673
from the pilot

00:05:16.673 --> 00:05:18.210
- Whenever we tell them to hold short

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.160
of runway two six right,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.010
I need for them to read
that exact phraseology

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.960
back which tells me that they understand

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.280
that that runway is active

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.690
and that they cannot cross that runway

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.350
until they get further clearance.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:30.183
(crosstalk)

00:05:30.183 --> 00:05:31.500
- [Narrator] But hearing
the correct read back

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.510
from the pilot isn't always enough.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.610
For example, on February 27th,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.130
an air traffic controller
instructed a pilot to-

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:38.963
- [Controller 2] Line up and wait,

00:05:38.963 --> 00:05:40.227
traffic rolling across the runway.

00:05:40.227 --> 00:05:41.250
- [Narrator] And the pilot read

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.750
back the instructions clearly

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.360
but began a takeoff role instead.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.210
Controllers also have
a lot of FAA guidelines

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.920
and regulations they need to follow.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.005
- And this is it.

00:05:51.005 --> 00:05:53.100
The 7110.65 is well,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.090
people like to call it our Bible.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.680
Controllers like to call it point 65,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.620
but this is everything that is required

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.420
for us in order to do our jobs

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.040
on a daily basis.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.810
- [Narrator] In light of
the recent close calls,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.420
the FAA held a safety summit in March.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.390
- Are we emphasizing
efficiency over safety?

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.970
- Initial information
suggests that more mistakes

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.300
than usual are happening
across the system.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.360
On runways, at gates
when planes are pushing

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.983
back, in control towers
and on flight decks.

00:06:21.983 --> 00:06:24.630
- [Narrator] The FAA issued
a safety alert a week later

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.930
calling on airlines to
exercise continued vigilance.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.180
Joint FAA industry groups have stepped

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.700
up efforts to analyze
and counter the trend

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.110
according to industry documents

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.670
and officials involved.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:38.100
Officials haven't issued emergency orders

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.510
or mandated other changes

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:42.090
to carrier operations
yet and they don't think

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.190
there are imminent risks to passengers.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.500
But safety experts say they are concerned

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.940
that potential collision risks

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.680
could continue to rise.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.020
Still, Buckman says
controllers are trained

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.630
to deal with unusual
situations, which can happen

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:55.650
from time to time.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.150
- There's always a plan B,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.650
there's always a plan C.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:06:59.700
You always have to be planning

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.150
for what's gonna happen next.

00:07:02.048 --> 00:07:04.798
(dramatic music)

